With the 'regular' hula hoop from the toy store, it can be quite a challenge to keep it high. That is why special fitness hula hoops have been designed that are thicker, larger and heavier than the original hula hoops.
A fitness hula hoop is a lot heavier than a traditional hula hoop. A fitness hula hoop normally weighs between 1,2 and 2,3 g.
In Canada, at the University of Waterloo, scientists have even... research done.
It turned out that after six weeks, on average, there was a significant reduction in both hip and waist size. Waist circumference had decreased on average by 3,4 cm, and hip circumference by 1,4 cm.
What are the benefits of hula hooping?
Because you train your abs through hula hoops, you will eventually get a tighter stomach.
The older you get, the harder it sometimes becomes to lose fat in certain places on your body. The abdomen and waist are often the target.
In addition, it is also very good for your heart!
In terms of combustion, it is comparable to running.
The hip swaying movements you make while hula hooping will lead to more flexibility in the back and hips.
Result: less back pain. Hula hooping can also be very helpful for people who have jobs that require sitting all day, or much of their day.
Training with a trampoline is also fun!
If your trunk muscles are insufficiently developed, other muscle groups will take over the stabilizing function. This will put more pressure on the spine and can cause back problems and/or injuries.
You hardly need space for hula hoops. Do it in the evening in front of the TV or in the garden when the weather is nice!
While hula hooping your heart rate (so good to measure it with a sports watch). Hula hoops keep your heart healthy and strong.
You not only train your muscles, your brain is also challenged. You can turn the hoop in your natural direction, but of course also the other direction, so that your brain has to adapt.
Which fitness hula hoop do I need?
If you are 'blessed' with a set of strong abdominal and back muscles, then it is best to go for a slightly heavier hula hoop. If your lower back is weak, go for a lighter version.
To start, place the hoop against your back, at your waist and twist it to the right or left with speed. After that, the goal is to keep the hoop spinning.
