Go and Crush it!
This next level covers much more material and you need to be more prepared than before.
By getting this overview done early, you will know what you have ahead of you with plenty of time to accomplish it.
If the topic you’re struggling with is the highest weighted topic, you should get studying much sooner.
Before you start studying, figure out what CFA® test prep materials you want to use and plan out how many hours a day you will set aside for studying.
My study routine came out to about 5 hours on weeknights and 16-20 hours on the weekends.
I can not emphasize enough how important the practice exams are, so make sure you have plenty of time to take these and learn where you are making mistakes.
Many of the concepts in Level II build on the basic concepts tested in Level I. That’s why it’s essential to understand concepts instead of memorizing information to spit out on the exam.
Some test prep materials actually include a Level I refresher book specifically for this purpose.
Level II is formatted differently than Level I.
One of the item-sets contains a vignette (also known as a case statement) with 6 questions on what is discussed in the case.
Sadly, this is not necessarily true even though you will have twice the amount of time per question compared to Level I.
The vignette is carefully crafted to confuse you and make you spend additional time sifting through what is and isn’t relevant.
I also recommend reading the questions before the vignette so you know what to look for while reading.
The questions are now part of a 6 member family, meaning you will see nasty interdependent questions.
Not only are they connected, they will be more in depth since they can reference the vignette.
This won’t work for Level II.
I should also mention that the 6 vignette questions are worth twice the points of a question in Level I.
Here are the weightings for the topics:
Portfolio Management and Wealth Planning: 5-10%
If you stick to your study schedule and continue, you are going to kick-ass on Level II.
There are a lot of dates to keep track of when you are preparing to take the CFA® Exam.
So register as soon as you can in order to take advantage of extra study time with the topic tests.
These scholarships are role-based opportunities available to qualified individuals in the media, academic, and financial communities.
Keep the following dates in mind during your CFA® Exam preparation and you won’t run into any surprises.
Take a look at all the CFA® Review course options by clicking the button below – we compare CFA® review courses so you can find the best one for your needs!
You’re more than halfway there!
It should be easy from here…right?
I’m here to help you get past these so you can finally complete your CFA® exam process.
After years of self-motivated studying, your mental strength to keep going is not as strong as it used to be.
Look at it this way—if you stay strong and study right, this is the last time you are going to have to do this.
This will save you from getting caught up on exam day by the different format in the morning session, which includes essay-based questions.
For the calculation based answers, you will need to show all of your work to get as many points as you can.
The examiner is looking for key concepts, not a novel.
I strongly suggest you practice these essay questions, especially if writing is not one of your strengths.
But for the essay questions, this could be a big problem if you do not know the answer.
The questions will be filled with irrelevant information about clients’ names, birthdays, and numerical payments that you will have to sift through to find the question you really need to answer.
Once you’ve read the question, make sure you know exactly where to write your answer.
So if you have time to go back and double check your answers, make sure they’re in the right place too.
The finish line is in sight, so it’s natural to speed up so freedom comes faster.
