Similarly, the word Holy Spirit in the Greek comes from the combination of words pneuma hagion (πνεῦμα ἅγιον), which most literally means, “air in movement – that is sacred”. The Holy Spirit (Pneuma) is most literally the breath or wind of God was used to create the universe and that God uses to interact with man.
רוּחַ rûaḥ; from 7306; wind; by resemblance breath, i.e. a sensible (or even violent) exhalation; figuratively, life, anger, unsubstantiality; by extension, a region of the sky; by resemblance spirit, but only of a rational being (including its expression and functions): — air, anger, blast, breath, x cool, courage, mind, x quarter, x side, spirit((-ual)), tempest, x vain, ((whirl-))wind(-y).
The correlation between the Spirit and God’s breath or wind is also illustrated in the following verses:
By his wind the heavens were made fair.
But it is the Spirit in man, the breath of the Almighty, that makes him understand.
The wind blows where it wishes, and you hear its sound, but you do not know where it comes from or where it goes.
(3) a part of human personality, spirit
(5) God’s being as controlling influence, with a focus on association with humans, Spirit
