To put it simply, 3D printing is the process of “rapid prototyping”, creating objects of almost any shape from a digital model using an additive process, in which layers are piled on top of each other to achieve the physical form. Several products have been introduced to the market in the past weeks with the intention of simplifying the experience for both the consumers and printers.
If you rather go straight into creating your own 3D prototypes without the aid of an actual printer, then try the LIX 3D-Printing Pen. This is a small, slim and lightweight aluminium pen that runs on the energy of your laptop to heat and liquefy the filament, allowing solid air drawing.
The only program guiding the pen would be your hand, so you will have to move steadily as the melted filament drips out and dries to turn your structure rigid. Currently, the filament rod per pen will allow up to two minutes of air drawing.
They are currently sourcing for materials to develop the basis of their 3D printers, but can already promise that they will deliver 3D printer ready files for architecture, engineering, and product design so that it will be user-friendly. The printer support will be adapted to both English and Khmer.
